As a first step to study the thermochemistry and electronic structure of An-MOFs, we explored their chemical stability. For instance, we observe significantly different behavior of Th-MOFs $(\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\mathrm{-}}8$ or $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\bf-M e}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-NO}_{3}{\bf-}10)$ 0 versus $\mathrm{U_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ , which could be partially explained by a wide range of accessible oxidation states in the latter case.
Crystals of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\bf-M e}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\bf-}8$ were immersed in water and changes in crystallinity were monitored by PXRD. We found that $\mathrm{\Th}_{6}{\bf-M e}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\bf-}8$ remained stable in water for three months, the longest time reported for any An-MOFs, rendering $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\bf-M e}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\bf-}8$ as one of the most water stable An-MOFs reported to date (Figure 2). Following this result, the stability of $\mathrm{U}_{6}–\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}–8$ was also explored. Crystals of $\mathrm{U}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}8$ were soaked in water for a week under a nitrogen atmosphere. The uranium-based MOF retained its crystallinity under prolonged immersion in water preserving uranium in the $+4$ oxidation state, a unique feature which was only achieved due to the fact that the framework was kept under an inert atmosphere. In air, $\mathrm{U}^{+4}$ transforms to $\mathbf{U}^{+6}$ based on XPS spectroscopic studies leading to framework degradation. In contrast, $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\mathrm{-}}8$ (Th has only one stable oxidation state, $+4$ ) does not require this specific precaution, and as expected, $\mathrm{Th^{+4}}$ was preserved in air. Under nitrogen atmosphere, $\mathrm{U_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ not only retains its crystallinity, but the single crystals were still suitable for single-crystal $X\cdot$ - ray diffraction analysis (Figure 2).
